:: Decision8
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 7>><<ownStats>><p class="charge">A commitment · no correctness points</p></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">A Knight Needs a Witness</div><h1>The introduction I can sign</h1><p class="speaker">Don Quixote</p><div class="story-beats"><p>Quixote stands by the gate, expecting a sentence large enough to march under. I am holding the small beginning I can actually justify. My first question is not completely answered. A name can help someone begin; it cannot do all the work of becoming.</p><p>I choose how to introduce him honestly—or how to handle the temptation to flatter him. My evidence and interpretation checkpoints have already earned their credit. His reaction will not change those points. The journal waits for an account I am willing to carry back.</p><p>Before I turn the page, I may add a private reflection: what did I discover, and what remains uncertain? The door on the cover is waiting. It requires understanding, not the knight’s approval.</p></div><div class="after-beats"><<ownPersonal "reflection">><<ownChoice "intro-tender" "I introduce a man seeking heroic purpose, whose books and names outrun his tested equipment.">>
<<ownChoice "intro-critical" "I introduce a would-be knight whose self-invention is exposed by ordinary details, while acknowledging the longing behind it.">>
<<ownChoice "intro-flatter" "I introduce an already perfect knight, omitting the evidence that contradicts the claim.">></div></section></div>

